  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\nIssued at 222 PM CDT SAT AUG 15 2026\n\nToday surface high pressure is present across the Northern Gulf\nCoast. This afternoon is similar to yesterday with another\nexample of a very hot, humid, and unstable airmass across Central\nAlabama as some storms are breaking through with diurnal heating.\nIn the upper levels, a ridge is centered across the Deep South\nwith weak flow across Central Alabama. At the surface, little\nchange is expected through the short term (today through Sunday).\nHowever, aloft, the ridge is expected to expand westward and\nremain that way through Tuesday. This should help to suppress\noverall convection toward more isolated storms, and it will\nreinforce the heat over us. In the near term, any storms that do\npop up this afternoon and evening (mainly southeast counties)\ncould grow quickly with gusty winds and brief heavy rainfall with\nour unstable airmass.\n\nOutside of any storms, the heat will continue to be the main\nstory. The prolonged period of  heat will have an effect on folks\nwhether or not the heat index goes to 109 or 110. A heat advisory\ncontinues for all of Central Alabama for today through Monday.\nThere will not be any major features to help to cool the area off\nin the short term, so look for a high probability of heat\nadvisories to be continued through the beginning of this week.\nThe majority of areas should see max heat indices of 105 to 109.\nWhile there could be an isolated 110 plus at times, coverage of\nhigher indices should be isolated. There is a medium to high\nchance that some areas (especially west) could see triple digit\nhigh temperatures this afternoon into the beginning of next week.\nBy mid week, a surface boundary is expected to move into Alabama\nas the eastern portion of the upper ridge weakens. While this\nboundary is not going to bring cool weather to the state, we could\nsee a few slightly lower dew points and high temperatures at a \ncouple of points across portions of the area. This in turn could \nlower our heat risk, which we desperately need.\n\n08",
